{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Single-source the ticket-type map and extend the one-literal guard to cover it\n\n## Context\n\nThis is the SAME defect class as the just-closed bug fe15-3bc4-ed70-4b61, still live in a third\nmap — and the guard that was supposed to prevent recurrence does not cover it.\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py` enforces \"exactly one dict\nliteral defines each value map within `adapters/`\" for the STATUS map\n(`test_exactly_one_status_map_literal_within_adapters`, the assert at `:300`) and the PRIORITY map\n(`test_exactly_one_priority_map_literal_within_adapters`, the assert at `:308`). There is no such\nassertion for the TICKET-TYPE map, and it is duplicated:\n\n* `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:88` — `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE: dict[str, str] = {...}`, read at\n  `:129` for the Cloud create path.\n* `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:43` — `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE = {\"task\": \"Task\", \"story\": ...}`,\n  read at `:61` for the DC create path.\n\nBoth were verified present in the tree. `config.py:11` also references a `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` in a\ncomment, which is worth checking for a third copy while doing this.\n\n### Every consumer of the symbol, enumerated\n\nA repo-wide `git grep -n _LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` (string grep, not just a semantic reference search —\nthe LSP does not see string/attribute access reliably) returns exactly seven sites, and the plan\naccounts for all of them:\n\n1. `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:88` — definition (Cloud). Moves.\n2. `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py:129` — Cloud create-path read. Unchanged if the alias is kept.\n3. `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:43` — definition (DC). Moves.\n4. `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:61` — DC create-path read. Unchanged if the alias is kept.\n5. `rebar_reconciler/config.py:11` — a COMMENT only, no literal. Confirms there is no third copy in\n   config; the finding is recorded here so the check need not be repeated.\n6. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py:8` — a\n   docstring mention. No code impact.\n7. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py:87` —\n   **a live external consumer**: `assert \"session_log\" not in _of._LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE`, where `_of`\n   is the `outbound_fields` module. This reads the name as a MODULE-LEVEL ATTRIBUTE on\n   `outbound_fields`. If the relocation leaves the canonical map bound only under a new name (e.g.\n   `LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA`) or referenced inline, this test breaks with `AttributeError`.\n\n**Required binding strategy (this is not optional):** in `outbound_fields.py`, import the canonical\nmap under the historical private name, exactly as J2 did for the other two maps at\n`outbound_fields.py:35-36`:\n\n```python\nfrom rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira_family import LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA as _LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE\n```\n\nThat keeps `outbound_fields._LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` resolvable, so site 7 and the create-path read at\nsite 2 need no edit. The DC backend gets the same treatment for its own call site. If the\nimplementer instead chooses to rename, then site 7 MUST be updated in the same change and the suite\nre-run to prove it — the choice is free, leaving site 7 broken is not.\n\n## Why it matters more than it looks\n\nThe two copies are read by the two CREATE paths. A divergence means the same local ticket type\nbecomes a different Jira issue type depending on deployment — silently, at creation, with no\ncomparison anywhere that would notice. fe15's status map had already drifted by one key\n(`\"deleted\"`) before anyone looked; there is no reason to assume this one is luckier, and nothing\nwould tell us.\n\n## Approach\n\nFollow the **J2** pattern (story 62d9-9f6d-ecf4-4743), which is the established local idiom for a\nmap that lives on BOTH sides of the `adapters/` boundary and was reviewed when it landed: relocate\nthe single definition into `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py` alongside `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` and\n`LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA`, have both create paths import it under an alias, then extend the boundary\nguard so a future third copy fails the build.\n\nNote explicitly that fe15 is NOT the precedent to copy for the mechanism, only for the defect class.\nfe15 deliberately used a **parity test rather than a shared import**, because its third copy lived in\n`rebar_reconciler/config.py` — a zero-dependency core module that must not import a vendor adapter\npackage. That constraint does not apply here: both copies of the TYPE map already live under\n`adapters/`, so the shared import J2 used is available and is the right move.\n\n**Compare the two copies BEFORE unifying them.** If they have already drifted, the unification is a\nBEHAVIOUR change for one deployment and needs the divergence decided explicitly and recorded — the\nway J2 decided `\"deleted\" -> \"Done\"` — not silently resolved by whichever copy is moved.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py` — the single definition (and its export from the package\n  `__init__`, matching how the status/priority maps are exposed).\n- `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` — delete the literal, import under the `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE`\n  alias.\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` — delete the literal, import under the same alias.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py` — extend the one-literal guard\n  to the type map.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py` — touched ONLY\n  if the alias strategy above is not followed; unchanged if it is.\n- the characterization pins for the type mapping on both create paths (see Testing) — **this ticket\n  owns them unconditionally**.\n\n## Testing\n\n* the guard must FAIL when a second literal is reintroduced. \"Mutation-checked\" here means a\n  concrete manual mutation, not a mutation-testing tool: re-add a literal\n  `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE = {\"task\": \"Task\", \"story\": \"Story\", \"bug\": \"Bug\", \"epic\": \"Epic\"}` to\n  `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py`, run the boundary-guard module, record that the new test is\n  RED (and note the pass/fail counts), revert the mutation, record that it is GREEN again.\n* both create paths keep producing their current issue-type values, pinned BY VALUE — an assertion\n  on the built `\"issuetype\"` field for each of `task`/`story`/`bug`/`epic` through the Cloud create\n  path and through the DC create path, not an assertion on the map's shape.\n* `test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py` still passes unmodified (the alias check).\n* the full `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` suite is green after the relocation.\n\nOwnership note: sibling story cedc (DC backend characterization suite) also touches DC create-path\ncharacterization. To remove the ambiguity, the type-mapping pins are **owned here**; cedc depends on\nthis ticket and should reuse these pins rather than re-author them.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the two existing copies are DIFFED and the result is recorded in a comment on this ticket\n- [ ] if the two copies diverge, the chosen value is decided explicitly with its rationale recorded,\n      not defaulted to whichever copy was moved\n- [ ] exactly one `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE`-equivalent literal exists under `adapters/`, and it lives in\n      `adapters/jira_family/value_maps.py`\n- [ ] both create paths (`adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`, `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py`)\n      read that single definition, imported under the `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` alias\n- [ ] `outbound_fields._LOCAL_TO_JIRA_TYPE` still resolves as a module attribute, proven by\n      `test_outbound_differ_session_log_exclusion.py` passing UNMODIFIED — or, if the implementer\n      renamed instead, that test is updated in the same change and passes\n- [ ] the boundary guard covers the ticket-type map\n- [ ] that guard is MUTATION-CHECKED by the concrete procedure in Testing, with the RED and GREEN\n      verdicts recorded in a comment\n- [ ] the type mapping is pinned by value for the Cloud create path, for all four local types\n- [ ] the type mapping is pinned by value for the DC create path, for all four local types\n- [ ] the `config.py:11` comment reference is checked for a backing third copy, and the finding is\n      recorded either way"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785644101988672001,"uuid":"ec98c9ee-e1cb-40e0-addb-d30d383b3c74"}